{
  "voice_id": "tnSpp4vdxKPjI9w0GnoV",
  "model_id": "eleven_v4_turbo",
  "text": "These are ten thousand coffee shop wait times... and they're lopsided. Most people wait a few minutes. But about one in twenty waits half an hour or more. The average wait is ten minutes, and the spread is about ten minutes too. Now pick thirty people at random, and average their waits. That one average becomes one block in a new chart. Pick thirty more... and thirty more. After two thousand samples, the averages have piled up into a bell, centered on ten. That's the Central Limit Theorem. The waits stay lopsided. Only their AVERAGES turn normal.\n\nAnd it isn't about wait times. Die rolls are flat, and this population has two humps. Average thirty from either one... and the same bell appears.\n\nYou'll hear that thirty is always enough. That's a rule of thumb, not a law. A heavy tail, like incomes, is still lopsided at thirty. It can take HUNDREDS before the bell shows.\n\nSo what does sample size control? The spread. They all center on ten, but bigger samples pile up tighter. Samples of five spread about four and a half minutes. Thirty, under two. And a hundred, about one. The spread is the population's spread over the square root of n. Four times the data halves it.",
